Z||000045||Tim||11-15-1999||10:25 PM||||First- Assume the dude has a problem, either on his end or with you Tom.  Assume he wants to make it right, always give someone the change to make something right.  Find out what the problem is, the problem maybe a misunderstanding.<P>Second- Try to contact him in all ways, even snail mail, asking what the problem is.  Give him a reasonable time limit to contact you.  He maybe a "hard-pay".  I've found professional people are very slow to pay.<P>Last- In your last contact explain you will go public with your side, to protect others from getting ripped-off.<P>Also-  Are you sure he received the parts, are you sure he didn't sent the payment.  I think Tom is showing good sense in holding his tongue, the guy maybe a lawyer or a hit-man. Yes, there is a difference.  I hear the best lawyer jokes from my lawyer friends.||||reg||1
